    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>After a heavy defeat by Drusus the elder around 9 BCE, the Marcomanni moved under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroboduus">Maroboduus</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boiohaemum">Boiohaemum</a>, a land the Boii had held, whose name is the origin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroboduus">Maroboduus</a> built a Rome-aligned Suebian empire, but his refusal to back the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a> in 9 CE split the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a>, and he was deposed in 19 CE and died in exile at Ravenna.</li><li>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcomannic-wars">Marcomannic Wars</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> the Marcomanni and Quadi invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> itself, before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> stationed 20,000 men in each of their countries by 180.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/velleius-paterculus">Velleius Paterculus</a> wrote that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroboduus">Maroboduus</a> drilled his Bohemian soldiers almost to Roman standards and could call upon 70,000 experienced infantry and 4,000 cavalry.</p></aside>
    </section>

