    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The name Marcomanni probably comes from a Proto-Germanic word reconstructed as *markō, “border”, making them “border men”; which frontier it recalled is unknown. They first appear among the confederates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariovistus">Ariovistus</a> whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> defeated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> in 58 BCE, drawn up by tribe beside the Harudes, Tribocci, Vangiones, Nemetes, Sedusii and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a>. Their homeland east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> is uncertain: one common proposal is the Mainfranken region of northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, and archaeology links them and their Suebian neighbours with the Grossromstedter culture of the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a>.</p><p>Under Augustus the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a>, and Florus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orosius">Orosius</a> report that Drusus the elder almost wiped out the Marcomanni in his campaigns of 12–9 BCE and raised a mound of their spoils. The Marcomanni then moved, under their new king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroboduus">Maroboduus</a>, who had grown up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boiohaemum">Boiohaemum</a>, plains surrounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hercynian-forest">Hercynian Forest</a> where the Boii had lived. The name is the origin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> wrote that their territory “was won by valour”, and by 6 BCE Maroboduus had established a powerful kingdom there.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Archaeology of the Bohemian settlement, including cremation and inhumation burials, hints at a stratified society that gave special importance to its warrior class. The Plaňany group that developed in the old Boii lands shows the influence of the earlier Celtic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-tene-culture">La Tène culture</a>, and the wider Grossromstedter culture drew on the Jastorf culture of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and the Przeworsk culture of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. The Marcomanni are archaeologically hard to tell apart from other Suebian groups such as the Quadi and Hermunduri.</p><p>After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannius">Vannius</a> in 50/51 CE the Germanic population just north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> grew, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, and the region received increasing imports from the empire; Slovak archaeologists place a core area of the Vannian kingdom in the fertile lowlands around Trnava. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> supported their kings with arms and, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> put it, “more frequently by our money”. Long periods of peace and prosperity alternated with war. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcomannic-wars">Marcomannic Wars</a> some Marcomanni were settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and other parts of the empire, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallienus">Gallienus</a> settled others in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> Superior, and by the late 4th century at least some had become Christians.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/velleius-paterculus">Velleius Paterculus</a> wrote that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroboduus">Maroboduus</a> drilled his soldiers almost to Roman standards and could call on 70,000 experienced infantry and 4,000 cavalry, probably not all Marcomanni, and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> feared he might invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. In 6 CE Augustus sent two army groups under Sentius Saturninus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> against the Marcomanni in a pincer movement, but a revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> stopped the campaign. In 17 CE war broke out between the alliances of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a> and Maroboduus, and after an indecisive battle Maroboduus withdrew into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. In 89 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a>, after killing their peace envoys, was defeated by the Marcomanni.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcomannic-wars">Marcomannic Wars</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> were longer and bloodier. In 167 or 169 the Marcomanni and Quadi crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, destroyed Opitergium and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a>. Marcus Aurelius made his headquarters at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnuntum">Carnuntum</a>, and in 179 Marcus Valerius Maximianus won a decisive battle at Laugaricio (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trencin">Trenčín</a>). By 180 Roman garrisons of 20,000 men each were stationed among the Marcomanni and the Quadi. In 395 the Marcomanni crossed the frozen Danube with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, Alans and others, setting their wagons around themselves like a wall as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stilicho">Stilicho</a> approached.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Marcomanni were ruled by kings. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> wrote around 100 CE that they and the Quadi had kings of their own nation, descended from the noble stock of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroboduus">Maroboduus</a> and Tudrus, but that their strength depended on Roman influence. Maroboduus, who had lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and been favoured by Augustus, ruled from his royal seat Buiaimon. By <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>'s account his empire included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugii">Lugii</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semnones">Semnones</a> and peoples such as the Zumi, Butones, Mugilones and Sibini, and by 5 CE the Langobardi; Velleius said he subjugated all his neighbours by war or treaty.</p><p>Rome sought to control their leaders. After Maroboduus fell, his and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catualda">Catualda</a>'s subjects were moved to land between the Morava and the Cusus rivers under the Quadian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannius">Vannius</a>, who grew rich and unpopular and was overthrown in 50/51; his nephews <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vangio">Vangio</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sido">Sido</a> divided his realm as Roman client kings. In the 160s Ballomarius, king of the Marcomanni, led a peace mission to the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallienus">Gallienus</a> took Pipa, daughter of the Marcomannic king Attalus, as a concubine, and after 395 the Christian queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritigil">Fritigil</a> corresponded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose of Milan</a>, who began a peace treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stilicho">Stilicho</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Marcomanni left their mark on the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, through the Germanic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boiohaemum">Boiohaemum</a>, and for a time their kingdom was the only part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> did not control before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-teutoburg-forest">Battle of the Teutoburg Forest</a> in 9 CE. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a> sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroboduus">Maroboduus</a> the head of the defeated general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-quinctilius-varus">Publius Quinctilius Varus</a>, Maroboduus sent it on to Rome, and his refusal to join the revolt split the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a> into two warring alliances. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcomannic-wars">Marcomannic Wars</a> led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> to consider a new imperial province called Marcomannia, a plan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commodus">Commodus</a> dropped after his father's death in 180.</p><p>Their descendants passed into many peoples. Marcomanni were settled inside the empire and served throughout it: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/notitia-dignitatum">Notitia Dignitatum</a> of about 420 lists a tribune of the Marcomanni under the dux of northern Noricum and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> I, palatine units of Marcomanni in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and Equites Marcomanni in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a>, probably including Marcomanni, went west and founded the Kingdom of the Suebi in Gallaecia; others joined the empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> and the Alemanni, and it is very likely that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarians">Baiuvarii</a> included Marcomanni.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the 3rd century the centre of Suebian activity on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> shifted east, reflecting the rising importance of the Quadi and the decline of the Marcomanni. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracalla">Caracalla</a> boasted around 214/215 of his enmity with the Marcomanni, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a> believed they paid tribute to the Gothic king Ostrogotha, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> claimed a triumph over them in 299. After the Roman defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adrianople">Battle of Adrianople</a> in 378 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, Alans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> came to dominate the Middle Danube. The Rugii and Heruli may already have moved into the Marcomanni's old lands.</p><p>In 395 the Marcomanni joined the great raid across the Danube, after which Claudian wrote that the lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and the Adriatic were like deserts. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/notitia-dignitatum">Notitia Dignitatum</a> of about 420 gives the last clear trace of a Marcomannic polity, probably now south of the Danube. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-the-deacon">Paulus Diaconus</a> later listed the Marcomanni among the peoples <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> could call upon. Modern scholars doubt they were still distinguished from other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a>. After Attila's death in 453 a Suebian kingdom under Hunimund and Alaric appeared in or near north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Marcomanni were a Suebian Germanic people who settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroboduus">Maroboduus</a> around the turn of the era and lived for four centuries beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> frontier, alternating long periods of peace with wars that peaked in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcomannic-wars">Marcomannic Wars</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a>. By the 5th century they had passed into the empire, the Hunnic world and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a>.</em></p>

